WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.700
Objection: "Nice idea, but it likely won't happen." Response: It already did.

2
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:13.920
The Oxford RECOVERY trial tested seven treatments in six months, saved one million lives at five hundred dollars per patient.

3
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:19.660
The F.D.A.'s version costs forty-one thousand dollars per patient for the same information.

4
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.260
The trial used existing hospital systems. Hospitals, it turns out, already contain sick people.

5
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:34.200
This was apparently news. Follow-up: "But globally?" Response: The internet scaled globally.

6
00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.120
So did smartphones. So did TikTok, which is considerably less useful.

7
00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:47.280
This just scales what already worked. "We Need the Military Budget" Objection: "We can't reduce military spending.

8
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.060
That would make us vulnerable." Response: The treaty takes one percent.

9
00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:59.380
You keep ninety-nine percent. If you can't defend yourself with ninety-nine percent of history's largest military budget,

10
00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:06.980
the problem isn't money. The Pentagon literally cannot account for two point five trillion dollars in spending.

11
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.640
You're asking them to redirect money they already lost in the couch cushions.

12
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:16.260
Every nation reduces by one percent, so relative military balance stays proportional.

13
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:21.740
Everyone turns the volume down together. Nobody gets quieter relative to anyone else.

14
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.300
The greatest threats today aren't tanks rolling over borders.

15
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:32.680
They're microscopic things rolling through airports. COVID-nineteen killed more Americans than World War One, World War

16
00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:40.660
Two, Korea, Vietnam, Iraq, and Afghanistan combined. It did this without triggering a single defense system.

17
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:47.900
Your nearly nine hundred billion dollar military budget watched it happen, fully armed and completely useless.

18
00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:53.180
Broke, sick countries start wars. Healthy countries start businesses.

19
00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:59.520
Curing disease is preventative defense with better R.O.I. than any missile system ever built.

20
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.380
Follow-up: "But what if enemies don't reduce?" Response: That's what a treaty is.

21
00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:11.340
Everyone signs at once. Those who don't simply miss Incentive Alignment Bond benefits.

22
00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:15.140
Their politicians lose campaign funding and post-office opportunities.

23
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:21.110
Participating nations' politicians get rewarded instead. Self-interest does the heavy lifting.

24
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:28.500
It always does. "Big Pharma Will Block This" Objection: "Pharmaceutical companies will kill this.

25
00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.740
They profit from disease." Response: Pharma makes *more* money under this model.

26
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:40.880
Watch their opposition evaporate. Current system: Pharma pays two point six billion dollars per drug.

27
00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:49.520
Ninety percent fail. Only blockbusters profit. The business model is "spend billions gambling, hope one pill pays for the

28
00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.740
nine that didn't work." New system: The treaty fund pays pharma to run trials.

29
00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:00.560
Trials become revenue, not cost. More drugs become profitable, even the weird ones.

30
00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:08.220
Predictable income instead of pharmaceutical roulette. Pharma would lobby FOR this harder than they've ever lobbied for

31
00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:12.740
anything. You'd be giving drug companies more money to do what they already do.

32
00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:18.380
They'd trample each other getting to the signing ceremony. "What About National Sovereignty?"

33
00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:19.080
Objection: "This forces countries to spend money a certain way.

34
00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:22.560
That violates sovereignty." Response: Sovereignty matters. Countries should decide how to spend their own money.

35
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:29.100
That's exactly what this is. No country is forced to sign. No country is penalized for not signing.

36
00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.860
Countries that join get access to the treaty fund and bond benefits.

37
00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:39.180
Countries that don't simply miss out. Citizens who want it vote for politicians who support it.

38
00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:44.080
Citizens who don't, don't. The treaty doesn't override sovereignty.

39
00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:49.620
It gives voters a specific thing to ask for and politicians a profitable reason to say yes.

40
00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:53.500
Follow-up: "What if our government doesn't want to?". Response: Then they don't sign.

41
00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.960
If enough citizens disagree with that decision, they vote in someone who will.

42
00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:01.080
That's sovereignty working exactly as designed. "The F.D.A.

43
00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:01.080
Exists for a Reason" Objection: "We need F.D.A. approval to ensure safety.

44
00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.880
You're being reckless." Response: Drug safety is genuinely important.

45
00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:09.000
Nobody wants another thalidomide. The question is whether the current approach to safety is actually safe.

46
00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:13.880
Phase one safety testing prevented thalidomide from reaching the U.S.

47
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.360
market. That saved roughly one thousand lives. Since nineteen sixty-two, F.D.A.

48
00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:28.000
drug lag has contributed to an estimated four to ten million American deaths from delayed access to treatments already proven

49
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.580
safe. That's four thousand to ten thousand deaths caused for every death prevented.

50
00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:39.500
The current system isn't cautious. It's the most dangerous option available.

51
00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:47.360
It just doesn't feel dangerous because the people it kills die quietly, in waiting rooms, rather than dramatically, in headlines.

52
00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:52.920
Under the decentralized framework, patients see real data on risks and benefits.

53
00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:58.120
Then they decide. Safety doesn't decrease. The paperwork does.

54
00:04:58.860 --> 00:04:58.920
"What If Countries Cheat?" Objection: "Countries will promise to reduce military spending but won't."

55
00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:01.020
Response: You're right not to trust governments to keep promises.

56
00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:07.220
Most treaties fail for exactly this reason. Trust is a terrible enforcement mechanism.

57
00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:15.740
Which is why this system doesn't use it. Politicians who comply receive Incentive Alignment Bond benefits: campaign funding

58
00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:20.580
and post-office career opportunities. Politicians who don't comply get nothing.

59
00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.680
Contributions are public on blockchain, so compliance is verifiable.

60
00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:29.140
Smart contracts route benefits automatically to compliant participants.

61
00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:35.880
Traditional treaties rely on trust and shame. This one makes compliance the career-maximizing choice.

62
00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:40.760
The system assumes the worst about human nature and designs around it.

63
00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:46.850
Your cynicism about politicians isn't an objection; it's the engineering requirement.

64
00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:48.750
"You Can't Cure Aging" Objection: "Aging is natural and inevitable.

65
00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:54.820
You can't fight nature." Response: Dying from infected teeth was "natural" until dentistry.

66
00:05:55.660 --> 00:06:02.370
Dying in childbirth was "natural" until medicine. Dying of smallpox was "natural" until vaccines.

67
00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:10.800
"Natural" just means "nobody's fixed it yet." Dying of old age is "natural" in the same way dying of anything is natural.

68
00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:20.220
You're just used to this one. You already replace everything that breaks: Hearts: replaced (you're basically a car at this

69
00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:25.000
point). Kidneys: replaced (or hook you to a machine thrice weekly).

70
00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:33.480
Blood: replaced (vampire economics, but medical). Bones: replaced (titanium is better anyway).

71
00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:43.620
Joints: replaced (your grandma is fifteen percent metal). Aging is damage accumulation with known repair pathways: Telomeres

72
00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:50.180
shorten: lengthen them (telomerase activation). Cells senesce: clear them (senolytic drugs).

73
00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:59.580
Proteins misfold: refold them (molecular chaperones). Mitochondria fail: replace them (mitochondrial transfer).

74
00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:06.380
DNA breaks: repair it (CRISPR, base editing). You went to the moon with slide rules.

75
00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:10.500
You can probably fix cells with A.I. Your body is a machine.

76
00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:16.140
Machines can be repaired. Aging is harder engineering, not magic.

77
00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:20.520
You keep confusing the two because it makes you feel better about not trying.

78
00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:26.840
I'm Just One Person. Objection: "My vote/investment/share won't matter.

79
00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:33.190
I'm too small.". Response: This needs two hundred and eighty million people.

80
00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:38.520
You tell ten. They each tell ten. Six degrees of sharing reaches millions.

81
00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:44.660
It's a pyramid scheme, except at the end everyone lives longer instead of losing their savings.

82
00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:51.180
Your contribution: vote (two minutes), buy bonds (ten minutes), share (fifteen minutes).

83
00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:59.040
Total: twenty-seven minutes. Potential return: fifty thousand extra hours of life if diseases get cured.

84
00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:04.040
That's the best trade you'll ever make, and you've made some terrible ones.

85
00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:10.740
Reform the System Instead. Objection: "Why not just reform the F.D.A.

86
00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:15.740
and N.I.H.?". Response: People have been trying for fifty years.

87
00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:20.530
Here's the scorecard: More funding? Tried. They bought more paperwork.

88
00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:26.820
Different leadership? Tried. Same results, fancier titles. New regulations?

89
00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:31.240
Tried. Now takes twenty years instead of seventeen. Progress.

90
00:08:32.179 --> 00:08:37.760
Reform bills? Tried. Lobbyists killed them in committee. Efficiently.

91
00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:41.940
You keep trying to teach a fish to climb a tree. The fish isn't broken.

92
00:08:42.679 --> 00:08:47.600
It's a fish. The system is hard to fix because it IS working.

93
00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:55.140
Just not for you. Defense contractors spend one hundred and twenty-seven million dollars yearly on lobbying and get nearly

94
00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:59.340
one trillion dollars in contracts. That's not a broken system.

95
00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:06.020
That's the most efficient system in American capitalism. They won't surrender it because you asked nicely.

96
00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:11.940
Don't reform the system. Build around it. This Is Politically Impossible.

97
00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:17.000
Objection: "No government will agree to this. Pure fantasy.".

98
00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.080
Response: Politicians follow money the way rivers follow gravity.

99
00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:27.860
The aim: make this the easiest, most profitable decision of their careers.

100
00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:33.660
The military-industrial complex spends one hundred and twenty-seven million dollars yearly on lobbyists.

101
00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:38.860
You can't beat them with moral arguments. (They're immune.) Beat them at their own game.

102
00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:45.820
Raise one billion dollars through VICTORY Bonds. Allocate six hundred and fifty million dollars for lobbying.

103
00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.740
That overwhelms the defense industry's one hundred and twenty-seven million dollars spend.

104
00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:56.430
Go to the same K-Street firms defense contractors use. Outbid them for their top talent.

105
00:09:56.980 --> 00:10:00.780
Lobbyists work for the highest bidder. Become the highest bidder.

106
00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:07.540
Politicians suddenly hear more about curing cancer and less about threats from countries they can't find on maps.

107
00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:16.040
This isn't fantasy. It's capitalism. Once two hundred and eighty million voters demand it and the money is behind it, refusal

108
00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:21.780
becomes career suicide. Politicians are very good at not committing career suicide.

109
00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:29.940
What If the Science Is Wrong? Objection: "What if we fund one hundred thousand trials and nothing gets cured?".

110
00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:36.660
Response: Worst case: you learn, with high certainty, one hundred thousand things that don't work.

111
00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:43.220
Edison found ten thousand ways not to make a lightbulb. You'd find one hundred thousand ways not to cure cancer.

112
00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:47.440
That's called science. You've been doing it for four hundred years.

113
00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:52.240
It's the only method you have that actually works. Maybe use it more.

114
00:10:53.300 --> 00:11:00.220
Still better than the current system: One hundred trials over seventeen years, learn nothing, many retire wealthy.

115
00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:05.680
N.I.H. funding safe research confirming water is wet. F.D.A.

116
00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:09.780
blocking trials because the paperwork had a typo on page eight hundred and forty-seven.

117
00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:16.280
The decentralized framework runs forty-four point one times more trials for the same budget.

118
00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:21.300
Even if ninety percent fail, you get over four times more cures than today.

119
00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:26.140
Failure at scale is still more productive than success at a standstill.

120
00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:36.680
I Don't Have Time Objection: "I'm too busy.". Response: Vote (two minutes), buy bonds (ten minutes), share (fifteen minutes).

121
00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:42.040
Total: twenty-seven minutes. You spent longer than that choosing what to watch on Netflix last night.

122
00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:50.080
If diseases get cured, you save hundreds of hours in doctor visits and sick days, plus potentially tens of thousands of

123
00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:55.620
hours of extra life. That's a one hundred and eleven thousand times return on twenty-seven minutes.

124
00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:01.920
This Is Unrealistic Objection: "This will never happen. You're naive about human nature.".

125
00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:10.120
Response: Your species has an impressive track record of calling things unrealistic, then doing them, then pretending you

126
00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:17.460
always knew they were possible. Things that were "unrealistic": Human flight (impossible for millennia, then two bicycle

127
00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:23.320
mechanics). Moon landing (J.F.K. did it in eight years with computers weaker than your toaster).

128
00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:27.420
Democracy (kings ruled for five thousand years, then didn't).

129
00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:35.420
Ending slavery (entire economies depended on it). Women voting (half the population was excluded, the other half thought

130
00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:39.380
this was fine). The internet (who needs computers talking to each other?).

131
00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:47.140
What's actually unrealistic: Spending two point seven two trillion dollars on weapons while sitting on thirteen thousand

132
00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:52.050
nuclear warheads. That's enough for one hundred and thirty extinction events.

133
00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:57.280
You only need one. Expecting different results from the same broken system.

134
00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:01.400
Thinking you'll survive the A.I. revolution without fixing incentives.

135
00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:05.800
Fifty-five million people die each year from treatable causes.

136
00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:11.640
That's the unrealistic thing. You just got used to it. "War Is Human Nature".

137
00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:15.960
Objection: "War is inevitable. Countries need militaries to survive.".

138
00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:23.380
Response: Several countries beg to differ. Switzerland: Two hundred plus years avoiding major wars.

139
00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:27.460
Surrounded by both World Wars (literally in the middle, made chocolate).

140
00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:33.680
G.D.P. per capita: ninety-three thousand dollars (not killing people is profitable).

141
00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:42.520
Defense spending: zero point seven percent of G.D.P. Life expectancy: eighty-four years (six point five years longer than

142
00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:50.880
Americans who spend five times more on "defense"). Costa Rica: Abolished its army in nineteen forty-eight (said "nah, we're

143
00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:56.780
good"). Still sovereign seventy-five plus years later (nobody invaded the country with no oil).

144
00:13:57.460 --> 00:14:01.500
Redirected military budget to education and health. Life expectancy matches U.S.

145
00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:06.600
at a fraction of the cost. Zero invasions since (turns out nobody wants to conquer happy, educated people).

146
00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:11.640
The pattern is not subtle: countries choosing peace get richer.

147
00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:18.160
Countries choosing war get poorer. Countries drained by disease are unstable and start fights.

148
00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:23.700
Countries investing in their people become too prosperous and comfortable to bother picking them.

149
00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:29.280
Nuclear weapons made territorial conquest largely obsolete in nineteen forty-five.

150
00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:35.560
You're still budgeting like it's nineteen forty-four. "All Wars on X Have Failed".

151
00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:40.750
Objection: "War on Drugs, War on Poverty, War on Terror all failed.

152
00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:46.720
This will too.". Response: Those were government wars using central planning.

153
00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:56.620
This uses markets. Spot the difference. Why government "wars" fail: They create bureaucracies that need the problem to exist.

154
00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:01.600
The War on Drugs needs drug crime. The War on Poverty needs poor people.

155
00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:08.900
The War on Terror needs enemies. Each one accidentally created a government department whose continued employment depends

156
00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:12.600
on the problem never being solved. It's impressive, really.

157
00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:20.780
The only war humanity won decisively was World War Two. And that was against other humans, which is admittedly your area

158
00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:25.800
of expertise. When you declare war on abstract concepts, the concepts win.

159
00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:30.960
Why the War on Disease is different: Uses markets, not ministries.

160
00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:35.580
Pays for outcomes, not process. No bureaucracy to preserve (smart contracts).

161
00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:41.060
Competition between solutions. Researchers paid for cures, not grants.

162
00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:46.100
You're not declaring war on disease. You're declaring peace with biology.

163
00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:50.000
Then letting markets optimize. Call it the Market for Health.

164
00:15:50.540 --> 00:15:55.400
It's less dramatic, but it might actually work. "This Sounds Like Bribery".

165
00:15:55.980 --> 00:16:00.020
Objection: "You're just bribing politicians. That's illegal and immoral.".

166
00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:05.560
Response: You're describing lobbying. And yes, the mechanics are identical.

167
00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:10.380
Same K Street firms, same campaign contributions, same revolving door.

168
00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:17.980
The difference is what it buys. Current lobbying buys one trillion dollars in defense contracts and a pharmaceutical system

169
00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:26.540
optimized for chronic treatment over cures. This uses the same mechanics, transparently, to fund clinical trials instead.

170
00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:30.280
Same tool. Opposite direction. The tool was never the problem.

171
00:16:31.340 --> 00:16:37.020
"It's Unenforceable". Objection: "Government promises to pay are unenforceable fantasy."

172
00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:43.220
Response: Courts are slow. Money is fast. This doesn't rely on courts.

173
00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:46.820
Break your word, the system automatically funds your replacement.

174
00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:50.720
Politicians understand this incentive structure instinctively.

175
00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:57.200
It's the only language they're truly fluent in. "You Can't Verify two hundred and eighty million People".

176
00:16:57.420 --> 00:16:58.480
Objection: "Impossible to verify two hundred and eighty million people online without fraud."

177
00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:06.619
Response: You already verify hundreds of millions of people for banking, government services, insurance, and online gambling.

178
00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:11.819
You can verify people for the purpose of not dying. It's the same technology.

179
00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:18.040
The stakes are just higher and the forms are shorter. Government I.D.s: National e-I.D.

180
00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:26.160
systems (Estonia, India, E.U.). Biometrics: Fingerprints and face scans ensure unique individuals.

181
00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:35.840
A.I. fraud detection: Algorithms spot suspicious patterns. Math verification: End-to-end verifiable voting with zero-knowledge

182
00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:42.820
proofs. This is a solved problem wearing an unsolved hat. "Why Not Just Use Philanthropy?".

183
00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:49.860
Objection: "If this matters, why not raise money from donors instead of complicated bonds?"

184
00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:55.800
Response: All philanthropy combined is a rounding error compared to government budgets.

185
00:17:56.540 --> 00:18:01.840
A massive campaign would cannibalize donations from health charities already doing critical work.

186
00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:09.250
You'd be stealing from cancer research to fund cancer research, which is impressively pointless even by your standards.

187
00:18:09.580 --> 00:18:16.780
The goal is tapping the multi-trillion dollar stream of wasted government spending, not reshuffling the charity tip jar.

188
00:18:17.780 --> 00:18:24.960
"How Do You Prevent Waste?". Objection: "How do you ensure money helps patients instead of funding bureaucracy?"

189
00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:32.680
Response: A decentralized F.D.A. model achieves eighty-two times lower cost per patient.

190
00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:37.900
Pragmatic trials: five hundred dollars. Traditional trials: forty-one thousand dollars.

191
00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:47.920
Same science. Eighty-two times less overhead. The N.I.H.'s RECOVER initiative spent one point six billion dollars over four

192
00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:56.640
years and produced no treatment recommendations. Zero. With that same budget, the decentralized model could have run thousands

193
00:18:56.640 --> 00:19:02.970
of trials for millions of patients. The N.I.H. managed to spend all that money learning nothing.

194
00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:07.080
That takes talent. "Why Not Just Increase Health Funding?".

195
00:19:08.300 --> 00:19:13.040
Objection: "Why cut military spending? Just allocate more money to health research."

196
00:19:13.900 --> 00:19:18.940
Response: Money isn't the only constraint. You can't print more physicists.

197
00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:26.700
(You've tried. It takes twenty-five years and a P.h.D. program.) Cutting one percent from military budgets doesn't just

198
00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:33.620
move dollars. It frees actual human brains from building weapons to curing Alzheimer's.

199
00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:38.200
Right now your smartest people are optimizing missile trajectories.

200
00:19:38.680 --> 00:19:43.360
Those same brains could optimize drug molecules. The brains don't care.

201
00:19:43.660 --> 00:19:47.680
They just go where the funding is. What About Defense Industry Jobs?

202
00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:52.040
Objection: "This will destroy millions of military industry jobs."

203
00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:59.900
Response: The U.S. contribution is less than zero point five percent of trillions the Pentagon can't account for.

204
00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:05.480
You're not destroying jobs. You're redirecting ghost money into real ones.

205
00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:10.180
Engineers building guidance systems can build medical imaging devices.

206
00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:17.900
Same skills, fewer explosions, more job satisfaction surveys where nobody ticks "my work kills people."

207
00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:24.220
This Violates Election Law Objection: "Foreign nationals funding U.S.

208
00:20:24.220 --> 00:20:27.540
elections is illegal, and your bonds sound like securities."

209
00:20:28.160 --> 00:20:33.000
Response: Hire good lawyers. Defense contractors figured this out decades ago.

210
00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:39.760
You can too. Separate legal entities in each country, zero coordination on political spending.

211
00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:45.520
Utility token structures with proper exemptions (Regulation S, Regulation A plus).

212
00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:50.140
No shared systems, staff, or communication between political entities.

213
00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:58.040
If Lockheed Martin can legally funnel money to politicians across fifty countries, you can legally funnel money toward not

214
00:20:58.040 --> 00:21:02.620
dying. This is an addressable legal challenge, not an existential one.
